WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.975
Previously on Practical Prophets... Israel took Jericho, but a hidden violation brought defeat at Ai. Joshua recovered, outmaneuvered Ai, and honored a treaty with Gibeon, even though its leaders had deceived him. Coalitions of Canaanite kings fought Israel in the south and north. The major campaigns were followed by a daunting admission: much of the land remained to be possessed. Now Joshua must divide the inheritance among the tribes, and Israel must learn how to live in it.

00:00:35.025 --> 00:00:38.675
Chapter 14. The Eighty-Five-Year-Old Scout.

00:00:39.775 --> 00:01:00.625
The work of assigning Canaan's land was conducted by a small group of leaders with enormous responsibilities: Eleazar the priest, Joshua son of Nun, and the heads of Israel's tribal families. The western territories were to be divided by lot, according to the command given through Moses.

00:01:00.805 --> 00:01:24.205
Nine tribes and half of Manasseh still awaited their shares. Reuben, Gad and the other half of Manasseh already possessed land east of the river. The arithmetic of the tribes required explanation. Joseph's descendants were counted as two tribes—Ephraim and Manasseh—while Levi was not given a continuous tribal territory.

00:01:24.385 --> 00:01:39.760
The Levites would receive cities in which to live, with surrounding land for their animals and possessions. In this way the allotments were to follow Moses' instructions. Then a familiar man approached Joshua at Gilgal.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:59.265
Caleb son of Jephunneh, the Kenizzite, had been with Joshua when the two of them argued against the ten fearful scouts at Kadesh-barnea. Caleb had not forgotten what Moses promised him. He reminded Joshua that he had been forty years old when Moses sent him to investigate Canaan.

00:01:59.445 --> 00:02:21.145
The other scouts had discouraged Israel, but Caleb had given his report honestly and remained faithful to God. Moses had sworn that the land Caleb had walked upon would belong to him and his descendants. Forty-five years had passed since that promise. Caleb was now eighty-five. And he had come to claim it.

00:02:21.325 --> 00:02:38.200
His speech was not one of retirement. God had kept him alive, he told Joshua, and he remained as strong for battle and movement as he had been on the day Moses sent him into Canaan. “Give me this hill country,” he said. He knew what lay there.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:57.505
It was the region of the Anakim, the formidable inhabitants associated with the scouts' fears, and its cities had been described as large and fortified. Caleb did not pretend the challenge would be easy. He expressed confidence that with God's presence he could drive them out.

00:02:57.685 --> 00:03:20.335
Joshua blessed him and gave him Hebron as his inheritance. The town had earlier been known as Kiriath-arba, named in connection with Arba, the great man among the Anakim. The book explains why Caleb received it: he had followed Israel's God fully. The episode brings the story almost back to where it began.

00:03:20.515 --> 00:03:43.190
Joshua and Caleb had once stood against an entire frightened generation, insisting that the land could be entered. Now the man who had shared Joshua's unpopular position stood before him, old in years but still asking for the difficult territory. The inheritance he requested was not the safe land behind the army.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:50.370
It was the hill country that had helped make the other scouts afraid. And the land had rest from war.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:04:09.420
Caleb's numbers—forty at the scouting mission, forty-five years since Moses' promise, and eighty-five at the time of his request—are an unusually explicit chronological anchor. His speech does not claim that the Anakim are imaginary or that fortified cities pose no danger.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:22.150
It claims that God's promise makes the hard task possible. The allocation by lot and the exceptional provision for Levi set the framework for the extensive geographic descriptions that follow.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:27.050
Chapter 15. Judah's Border, Caleb's Daughter.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:38.200
A conquest creates a problem the moment the fighting stops: who gets which land? Judah's inheritance was drawn in the southern part of Canaan.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:57.205
The description begins at the border of Edom near the wilderness of Zin, traces the southern edge from the Salt Sea through the ascent of Akrabbim and Kadesh-barnea toward the Brook of Egypt, then follows the coast. The Salt Sea and the lower Jordan formed its eastern side.

00:04:57.385 --> 00:05:15.910
Its northern border passed through places whose names would recur in the history of Israel: the Valley of Achor, the Valley of Hinnom near Jebusite Jerusalem, Kiriath-jearim, Beth-shemesh, Timnah, and the outskirts of Ekron. The Great Sea marked the west.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:38.440
It was an enormous stretch of territory to describe, and far from uniformly settled. Within it, Caleb received the personal allotment promised to him. He was given Kiriath-arba, the old name of Hebron, associated with Arba, father of Anak. Caleb had asked Joshua for the hill country where the Anakim lived.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:59.595
Now the book says what happened when he went to possess it. He drove out three of Anak's descendants: Sheshai, Ahiman, and Talmai. He then turned against the people of Debir, once called Kiriath-sepher, and offered an unusual reward. Whoever captured the city would receive his daughter Achsah in marriage.

00:05:59.775 --> 00:06:17.925
Othniel son of Kenaz, identified as Caleb's brother or close kinsman in the Hebrew phrasing, took the city. Caleb gave him Achsah. But Achsah's part of the story does not end with her marriage. She persuaded Othniel about asking her father for land.

00:06:18.105 --> 00:06:36.680
When she dismounted from her donkey before Caleb, he asked what she wanted. “A blessing,” she answered. The land she had received lay in the dry southern country; she wanted water as well. She asked for springs. Caleb gave her both the upper and the lower springs.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:54.385
The exchange is brief, but it makes one of the central problems of inheritance concrete. A parcel of land is not automatically a livable home. A family needs water. After Achsah, the narrative turns back to the wider tribal register.

00:06:54.565 --> 00:07:08.915
The list is extensive: settlements toward Edom in the Negev, settlements in the Shephelah or lowland, settlements toward the Philistine coast, towns of the hill country, and desert towns near the Salt Sea.

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:36.420
The Negev list includes Beersheba, Ziklag, Hormah, Moladah and other settlements; the lowland registers include Zorah, Adullam, Socoh, Azekah, Lachish, Eglon, Libnah and Mareshah; the coastal holdings are described through Ekron, Ashdod, and Gaza; the highland towns include Debir, Hebron, Carmel, Ziph, Beth-zur and Kiriath-jearim; the wilderness group includes En-gedi and the City of Salt.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:59.500
These are regional registers, not an account of new battles in each town. One final line qualifies the enormous territorial description. The people of Judah could not drive out the Jebusites living in Jerusalem. The two groups continued to inhabit the area together, says the account, into the writer's own time.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:09.955
Judah had received a boundary and a list of towns. That did not mean every person inside the boundary had accepted Israelite rule.

00:08:10.655 --> 00:08:31.205
The scene with Achsah gives the allotment chapters an unforgettable human scale. She asks for water because land in the Negev is useful only if a household can sustain itself there. The long town lists distinguish the Negev, lowlands, coast, highlands and wilderness—different ecologies, not interchangeable names.

00:08:31.385 --> 00:08:48.835
And verse 63 explicitly reports that Judah did not expel the Jebusites from Jerusalem, a crucial qualification to the earlier conquest summaries. The exact full town register belongs in the reference appendix for readers who wish to study the boundaries.

00:08:49.735 --> 00:08:52.885
Chapter 16. Joseph's Sons.

00:08:53.985 --> 00:09:12.635
The sons of Joseph had a complicated inheritance. In Israel's tribal system, Joseph's descendants formed two tribes, Ephraim and Manasseh. Their western territories lay through the central hill country, reaching from the Jordan Valley toward the Mediterranean.

00:09:12.815 --> 00:09:24.290
The description begins near Jericho, where the route climbed out of the Jordan Valley toward Bethel, then passed Ataroth and lower Beth-horon toward Gezer and the sea.

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:41.570
Within this larger area, Ephraim's boundary was traced from Atroth-addar and upper Beth-horon through Michmethath, Taanath-shiloh, Janoah, Naarah, and back toward Jericho and the Jordan. In the west, the Brook of Kanah led toward the coast.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:10:05.075
Ephraim also received particular towns inside the territory otherwise assigned to Manasseh. The shape of the inheritance was intricate. It was not a single uninterrupted block with a neat modern border. The book closes Ephraim's allotment with a familiar complication. The tribe did not drive the Canaanites out of Gezer.

00:10:05.255 --> 00:10:27.005
The Canaanites continued living among Ephraim and were made to perform forced labor. That sentence is important because it refuses to let a map become a fiction of complete possession. Ephraim held an inheritance, but another population remained inside it, subject to an unequal political arrangement.

00:10:27.185 --> 00:10:32.085
The conquest had not made the country's older inhabitants vanish.

00:10:32.785 --> 00:10:53.460
The geography of Ephraim and Manasseh overlaps in places: Ephraim had named towns within Manasseh's territory. The Canaanites of Gezer were not expelled; they became a laboring population under Ephraimite power. The text treats that as unfinished expulsion, not full compliance with the earlier commands.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:11:01.240
Its political consequence will matter when the book describes the tribes' continuing difficulties settling the land.

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:06.165
Chapter 17. The Daughters Who Claimed Their Share.

00:11:07.265 --> 00:11:28.115
When the land was divided, inheritance normally passed through a family's sons. Five sisters came before the leaders to insist that their family must not disappear from the register. Their father was Zelophehad, son of Hepher, descended through Gilead and Machir from Manasseh. He had no sons.

00:11:28.295 --> 00:11:47.545
His daughters were Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah, and Tirzah. The sisters' case was not new. They had brought it to Moses while Israel was still in the wilderness. The Torah records that God upheld their claim: a man without sons could pass his inheritance to his daughters.

00:11:47.725 --> 00:12:05.400
Now Joshua and Eleazar were dividing the land, and the women came forward again. They reminded the priest, the commander, and the tribal chiefs that God had commanded Moses to give them a share among their father's relatives. The leaders honored the ruling.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:28.205
The daughters received their inheritance alongside their male kin. This mattered in the arithmetic of Manasseh's allotment. The tribe received ten portions west of the Jordan, apart from its territory in Gilead and Bashan to the east. The daughters' inheritance was one reason the western allotment was divided as it was.

00:12:28.385 --> 00:12:42.585
The eastern land remained with the other descendants of Manasseh. The chapter then traces Manasseh's boundary through the central hills, around Shechem and En-tappuah, toward the Brook of Kanah and the sea.

00:12:42.765 --> 00:12:56.865
Ephraim lay to the south, Manasseh to the north; the northern edge reached toward Asher, and the eastern edge toward Issachar. Some towns lay as enclaves of one tribe among those of another.

00:12:57.045 --> 00:13:14.020
Manasseh's holdings included Beth-shean, Ibleam, Dor, En-dor, Taanach, and Megiddo, with their dependent towns, in territory overlapping Issachar and Asher. But Manasseh did not dislodge all the Canaanites from those places.

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:33.150
The older inhabitants resisted, and as Israel grew stronger, some were placed under forced labor rather than driven out. Then the descendants of Joseph confronted Joshua. Why, they asked, had he given them only one allotment when they were so numerous and so greatly blessed?

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:52.855
Joshua's answer did not offer them an easier land grant. If they were as numerous as they claimed, he said, they should go into the forested hill country and clear space for themselves in the territory of the Perizzites and Rephaim. The tribes objected that the hill country was not enough.

00:13:53.035 --> 00:14:13.285
And in the valley the Canaanites had iron chariots, including around Beth-shean and the Valley of Jezreel. Joshua did not deny the chariots. He insisted that the descendants of Joseph possessed the strength to expand into the forest and drive out the Canaanites despite their military equipment.

00:14:13.465 --> 00:14:29.115
They would not be confined to one narrow share simply because the fertile valley looked difficult to take. The same book that promises Israel the land shows Joshua refusing to make the work of possessing it effortless.

00:14:29.815 --> 00:14:49.240
The daughters of Zelophehad first appear in Numbers 27, and their inheritance receives further treatment in Numbers 36. Joshua 17 is the point where that legal ruling becomes an actual land allotment. The chapter also distinguishes possession, partial settlement, and forced labor.

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:59.820
The Joseph tribes' complaint is answered with a challenge to clear forest and confront Canaanites using iron chariots, not by redrawing the map on demand.

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:04.145
Chapter 18. The Surveyors of Shiloh.

00:15:05.245 --> 00:15:24.995
Israel's leaders gathered the entire congregation at Shiloh and set up the Tent of Meeting there. The camp had changed. The Ark and the sanctuary were no longer part of a people constantly on the move. Shiloh became the location from which the remaining inheritances were to be apportioned.

00:15:25.175 --> 00:15:42.400
Seven tribes were still waiting for their land. Joshua was impatient. “How long will you delay,” he asked them, “before going to possess the land that the Lord, the God of your ancestors, has given you?” He ordered each tribe to appoint three men.

00:15:42.580 --> 00:16:03.555
They were to travel through the remaining territory, survey it, describe it town by town, and return with a division into seven portions. Judah was to remain in its southern territory. The house of Joseph would remain to the north. The new survey would concern the land still awaiting allocation.

00:16:03.735 --> 00:16:23.685
Joshua would then cast lots before God at Shiloh. The Levites were excluded from the territorial division because the priesthood was their inheritance. And the eastern tribes—Reuben, Gad and half of Manasseh—had already received their allotments from Moses beyond the Jordan.

00:16:23.865 --> 00:16:44.465
The men set out and returned with something crucial: a written description of the country arranged into seven portions, naming its towns. Joshua cast the lots. The first inheritance in the new distribution fell to Benjamin. Benjamin's territory lay between Judah and Joseph's descendants.

00:16:44.645 --> 00:16:51.795
Its northern border rose from the Jordan near Jericho toward Bethel and the Beth-horon region.

00:16:51.975 --> 00:17:11.400
The western side reached Kiriath-jearim; its southern edge traced the region of the Hinnom Valley and the outskirts of Jebusite Jerusalem before returning by En-rogel, the Stone of Bohan, Beth-hoglah, and the northern end of the Salt Sea. The Jordan bounded it to the east.

00:17:11.580 --> 00:17:34.930
The towns included Jericho, Bethel, Geba, Gibeon, Ramah, Beeroth, Mizpeh, Chephirah, Zela, Gibeah, Kiriath, and the Jebusite city later known as Jerusalem. The chapter preserves two groups of towns, numbering twelve and fourteen, with their villages. The surveyors had turned a territorial promise into a document.

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:55.135
Joshua had used the lots to turn that document into tribal inheritances. But Shiloh's sanctuary remained the center of the transaction. The book wants the reader to understand that this was not simply the work of surveyors and administrators. The distribution took place before God.

00:17:55.835 --> 00:18:15.335
Shiloh's Tent of Meeting is a major development in Israel's institutional life. Joshua demands a written survey, with three men appointed from each tribe, and the territory is divided into seven portions before the casting of lots. The text carefully preserves Benjamin's boundaries and town groups.

00:18:15.515 --> 00:18:25.515
Its geographical detail reminds us that inheritance was a legal and communal assignment, not just a story of armies moving through the countryside.

00:18:26.415 --> 00:18:30.490
Chapter 19. Seven Lots and One Last Home.

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:49.390
The allotment at Shiloh continued. The second lot fell to Simeon, but the result was unusual. Simeon's territory lay within Judah's inheritance. Judah's portion had been too large, and Simeon received towns carved from its southern lands.

00:18:49.570 --> 00:19:10.695
The register lists Beersheba and Sheba, Moladah, Hazar-shual, Balah, Ezem, Eltolad, Bethul, Hormah, Ziklag, Beth-marcaboth, Hazar-susah, Beth-lebaoth, and Sharuhen, followed by Ain, Rimmon, Ether, and Ashan, with their surrounding villages as far as Baalath-beer and Ramah of the South.

00:19:10.875 --> 00:19:25.900
The list divides the towns into groups of thirteen and four. This was not an independent northern territory; Simeon's homes were embedded within Judah's southern allotment. The third lot went to Zebulun.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:40.605
Its boundaries were traced from Sarid through Maralah, Dabbesheth, the brook near Jokneam, Chisloth-tabor, Dobrath, Japhia, Gath-hepher, Eth-kazin, Rimmon-methoar, Neah, Hannathon, and the Valley of Iphtahel.

00:19:40.785 --> 00:20:02.185
Its named towns included Nahalal, Shimron, Idalah and a place called Bethlehem—not necessarily the Bethlehem in Judah. Issachar received the fourth lot. Its named area included Jezreel, Shunem, En-gannim, Beth-pazzez, Tabor, and Beth-shemesh, with sixteen towns and their villages.

00:20:02.365 --> 00:20:22.665
The fifth lot fell to Asher, whose coastal and northern boundaries ran through Helkath, Achshaph, Carmel, Beth-dagon, Cabul, Rehob, and the approaches toward Great Sidon and the fortified city of Tyre, eventually reaching the sea near Achzib. The register names twenty-two towns.

00:20:22.845 --> 00:20:40.145
Naphtali received the sixth lot, stretching through northern hills and toward the Sea of Chinneroth, with places such as Hammath, Rakkath, Chinneroth, Hazor, Kedesh, Edrei, and Beth-shemesh. The register names nineteen towns and their villages.

00:20:40.325 --> 00:21:00.400
The seventh lot went to Dan, in the region of Zorah, Eshtaol, Timnah, Ekron, Aijalon, Gath-rimmon, and the country toward Joppa. But Dan's assigned territory proved difficult to possess. Its people went north, fought against Leshem, captured it, and renamed it Dan after their ancestor.

00:21:00.580 --> 00:21:21.255
The territorial assignment had ended. Yet the book had one more inheritance to record. Only after the tribes received their portions did the Israelites grant a city to Joshua himself. He asked for Timnath-serah in the hill country of Ephraim. According to God's command, they gave it to him.

00:21:21.435 --> 00:21:38.735
Joshua built up the city and lived there. It was a restrained ending for the man who had led the crossing and the conquest. The chapter does not give him an empire or a dynastic throne. It gives him a town in the land he had helped Israel enter.

00:21:38.915 --> 00:21:53.940
Eleazar the priest, Joshua, and the heads of the tribal families had completed the casting of lots at Shiloh before the Lord, at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting. The great work of distribution was finished.

00:21:54.640 --> 00:22:15.015
These allotment lists are among the least novelistic portions of Joshua, but they carry several consequential facts. Simeon receives land inside Judah's territory, not a fully separate district. Dan's people cannot simply take their original allotted region and instead capture Leshem and rename it Dan.

00:22:15.195 --> 00:22:32.320
Joshua receives his own town only after the tribal allocations are completed. The full source register, including the smaller towns, is preserved separately for verification; no new scenes are implied by the literary arrangement of the geographic summaries.

00:22:33.220 --> 00:22:36.170
Chapter 20. A Place to Run.

00:22:37.270 --> 00:22:56.570
The land had been divided, but a society needs more than borders. It needs rules for what happens when people harm one another. God told Joshua to establish the cities of refuge that Moses had commanded. The principle answered a frightening situation in the ancient world.

00:22:56.750 --> 00:23:18.850
A person could kill another human being without planning to do so or harboring hatred. The dead person's relatives might still seek revenge. Without some protected place to run and some means of judging the case, an accidental death could set off another killing. The cities of refuge were meant to interrupt that chain.

00:23:19.030 --> 00:23:40.555
A person who killed unintentionally could flee to one of the designated cities. At its gate, before the city's elders, he was to explain his case. The elders would admit him and provide a place to live. If an avenger of blood pursued him, the city was not to surrender him merely to satisfy that demand.

00:23:40.735 --> 00:23:54.585
The protection rested on the distinction between intentional murder and an unintended killing committed without prior hatred. But refuge was not a declaration that the killing had never happened.

00:23:54.765 --> 00:24:07.440
The person would remain in the city until judgment before the congregation, and the law connected his eventual return home with the death of the high priest serving at that time.

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:21.295
Joshua set apart three cities west of the Jordan: Kedesh in the Galilee highlands of Naphtali, Shechem in Ephraim's hill country, and Kiriath-arba—Hebron—in Judah's hill country.

00:24:21.475 --> 00:24:39.375
Three more lay east of the river, where Moses had assigned territory to the other tribes: Bezer in Reuben's plateau, Ramoth in Gilead among Gad, and Golan in Bashan among Manasseh. Six cities, distributed across both sides of the Jordan.

00:24:39.555 --> 00:24:55.280
The protection applied not only to Israelites by birth but also to the stranger living among them. Anyone who killed unintentionally could flee there and avoid death at the hands of an avenger before the community heard the case.

00:24:55.460 --> 00:25:10.785
In the midst of a book filled with armies, fortifications, and the destruction of cities, Joshua 20 places a different kind of map before the reader: a map of places where the vulnerable could reach legal protection.

00:25:11.485 --> 00:25:31.210
The cities of refuge do not provide sanctuary to every killer. Their specific purpose is to protect someone who caused a death unintentionally, before the community can determine responsibility. The rule requires a hearing and distinguishes unintended killing from an act motivated by hatred.

00:25:31.390 --> 00:25:42.990
The presence of three cities on each side of the Jordan matters because Israel's settled territory now occupied both banks. The inclusion of resident strangers is explicit.

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:47.540
Chapter 21. The People Without a Province.

00:25:48.640 --> 00:26:04.240
Levi had received no ordinary tribal inheritance. That did not mean the Levites had nowhere to live. At Shiloh, the heads of the Levite families approached Eleazar the priest, Joshua, and the tribal leaders.

00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:26.045
They reminded them that God, through Moses, had commanded Israel to provide cities for the Levites, with surrounding land for their animals. The request was not charity. It was an unfinished provision of the covenant. The Israelites agreed and distributed cities from within their own tribal inheritances.

00:26:26.225 --> 00:26:42.125
The sons of Aaron, a branch of the Kohathite Levites, received thirteen cities from Judah, Simeon, and Benjamin. The first allotment included Hebron, also known as Kiriath-arba, with surrounding grazing land.

00:26:42.305 --> 00:26:57.730
There was a distinction important to Caleb's inheritance: the city's fields and villages belonged to Caleb, while the Levites received the city and its surrounding land. Hebron was also one of the cities of refuge.

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:08.035
Other priestly cities included Libnah, Jattir, Eshtemoa, Holon, Debir, Ain, Juttah, Beth-shemesh, Gibeon, Geba, Anathoth, and Almon.

00:27:08.215 --> 00:27:30.940
The remaining families of Kohath received ten cities from Ephraim, Dan, and western Manasseh, including Shechem—another city of refuge—Gezer, Kibzaim, Beth-horon, Elteke, Gibbethon, Aijalon, Gath-rimmon, and Taanach. Gershon's families received thirteen cities from Issachar, Asher, Naphtali, and eastern Manasseh.

00:27:31.120 --> 00:27:45.920
Their portion included Golan in Bashan, also a city of refuge, and Beeshterah, Kishion, Dobrath, Jarmuth, En-gannim, Mishal, Abdon, Helkath, Rehob, Kedesh in Galilee, Hammoth-dor, and Kartan.

00:27:46.100 --> 00:28:05.325
Merari's families received twelve cities from Zebulun, Gad, and Reuben, including Jokneam, Kartah, Dimnah, Nahalal, Ramoth in Gilead, Mahanaim, Heshbon, and Jazer. The list places the Levites throughout Israel rather than concentrating them in a single province.

00:28:05.505 --> 00:28:23.805
The sanctuary service belonged to them, but their homes would stand among the other tribes. The total given is forty-eight cities, each with its surrounding land. At the end of the distribution, the book steps away from the individual grants and looks at what has happened.

00:28:23.985 --> 00:28:45.610
God had given Israel the land promised to their ancestors. They possessed it and lived in it. He had given them rest from surrounding enemies; none had been able to stand against them. The good promises made to Israel, the writer declares, had all been fulfilled. It is one of the book's great statements of completion.

00:28:45.790 --> 00:29:07.465
But it appears in a narrative that has already acknowledged remaining inhabitants and unoccupied territory. The completion is being stated from within a covenantal summary, not as a claim that every later challenge has disappeared. The people had inherited a land. Now they had to sustain a way of living in it.

00:29:08.165 --> 00:29:27.015
The Levites are distributed among other tribes rather than given a continuous region. Joshua 21 counts forty-eight cities in all, divided among descendants of Aaron and the families of Kohath, Gershon, and Merari. Several cities simultaneously serve as cities of refuge.

00:29:27.195 --> 00:29:41.070
The chapter's concluding declaration that God's promises had not failed should be read alongside the earlier acknowledgment that much of the land remained to be possessed. Both statements belong to Joshua's own account.

00:29:41.970 --> 00:29:46.720
Chapter 22. The Altar That Nearly Started a Civil War.

00:29:47.820 --> 00:30:09.170
The eastern tribes had kept their promise. For years, the fighting men of Reuben, Gad, and half of Manasseh had remained with their fellow Israelites west of the Jordan. Now Joshua called them to him and acknowledged what they had done. They had obeyed Moses, he said, and had followed Joshua's instructions.

00:30:09.350 --> 00:30:25.350
They had not abandoned their brothers throughout the campaign. God had now given the western tribes rest, and the eastern men could finally return to the lands Moses had assigned them. Joshua did not send them home without a warning.

00:30:25.530 --> 00:30:43.430
They must continue to love God, walk in His ways, keep the commandments, hold fast to Him, and serve Him wholeheartedly. Then he blessed them. He also told them to take their considerable spoils home: livestock, silver, gold, bronze, iron, and clothing.

00:30:43.610 --> 00:31:04.285
These were to be shared with the brothers who had remained in the eastern territory. The men departed from Shiloh and made their way toward Gilead. Then they built an altar near the Jordan. It was large and conspicuous, the sort of structure that could not be overlooked. The news reached the western Israelites.

00:31:04.465 --> 00:31:25.690
Reuben, Gad, and half of Manasseh had built an altar in the Jordan region, apparently on the Canaan side of the river. The rest of Israel drew an alarming conclusion: the eastern tribes had erected a rival place of worship. The congregation assembled at Shiloh, ready to go to war against them.

00:31:25.870 --> 00:31:46.220
The danger was not only political division. Israel's law forbade unauthorized sacrificial worship, and the memory of collective punishment remained painfully vivid. Before attacking, the western tribes sent a delegation. Its leader was Phinehas, son of Eleazar the priest.

00:31:46.400 --> 00:32:08.025
Ten tribal princes accompanied him, each representing an Israelite family leadership group. They confronted the eastern tribes in Gilead. What treachery was this, they demanded? Had the eastern tribes turned away from God by building a rival altar? They invoked two national disasters as warnings.

00:32:08.205 --> 00:32:25.805
At Peor, Israel had sinned and suffered a plague. At Jericho, Achan's secret theft had brought punishment upon the whole nation. The principle was clear: one group's rebellion could endanger everyone. The delegation even offered a way out.

00:32:25.985 --> 00:32:48.085
If the eastern territory was somehow unfit for worship, the tribes could cross westward and settle among their brothers where the Tabernacle stood. But they must not build an altar for sacrifice other than the one associated with God's sanctuary. The accused tribes answered with an emphatic appeal to God as witness.

00:32:48.265 --> 00:33:08.140
If they had built the structure for rebellion, or to offer burnt offerings, grain offerings, or peace offerings there, then God should judge them. But that was not why they built it. They had been afraid of something else. The Jordan formed a physical boundary between their descendants and the western tribes.

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:25.320
One day, they feared, children living west of the river might tell their children in the east that they had no share in Israel's God. The river could become more than a border; it could become a theological division. The altar was meant to prevent that.

00:33:25.500 --> 00:33:48.600
It was a replica, a visible reminder of the one legitimate altar, not a rival place for sacrifices. Its purpose was to serve as a witness between the tribes and their descendants, proving that the eastern families belonged to the same covenantal people. Phinehas and the princes listened. The explanation satisfied them.

00:33:48.780 --> 00:34:10.130
Phinehas declared that they now knew God was among Israel, because the eastern tribes had not committed the rebellion of which they were suspected. Their explanation had saved the nation from the danger of divine punishment. The delegation returned west and reported what they had heard. The Israelites were relieved.

00:34:10.310 --> 00:34:26.660
They blessed God and abandoned their preparations for war. The altar was left standing, not as a threat, but as a witness that the Lord was God. A civil war had nearly begun over a structure intended to keep Israel united.

00:34:27.360 --> 00:34:48.685
Joshua 22 is a remarkable example of a near-war prevented by inquiry. The western tribes have reason to fear unauthorized worship, particularly after the disasters of Peor and Achan. The eastern tribes, meanwhile, fear that the river will one day exclude their descendants from Israel's religious identity.

00:34:48.865 --> 00:35:04.240
Their altar is described as an ed, a witness, not a place for offerings. The episode's turning point is that Phinehas listens to the explanation and accepts it. The resolution depends on distinguishing intent from appearances.

00:35:05.140 --> 00:35:08.215
Chapter 23. The Last Warning.

00:35:09.315 --> 00:35:31.115
Years passed. The wars that had dominated Joshua's life receded. Israel had been given rest from the surrounding enemies, and Joshua himself had grown old. He summoned the nation's elders, heads, judges, and officers. He began not by celebrating himself but by reviewing what they had witnessed.

00:35:31.295 --> 00:35:50.495
God, Joshua said, had fought for Israel. The nations defeated in the campaigns had not fallen because Joshua possessed some special power independent of the covenant. He had already distributed the land by tribe, including the territories of peoples who still remained.

00:35:50.675 --> 00:36:08.950
God would continue driving those remaining peoples away so that Israel could possess the land fully. But the future depended on the people themselves. They must be courageous in observing everything written in Moses' Torah, turning from it neither right nor left.

00:36:09.130 --> 00:36:29.180
They must not adopt the worship of the remaining nations, invoke the names of those gods in oaths, serve them, or bow before them. Instead they were to hold fast to Israel's God. Joshua reminded them that great nations had fallen before comparatively small Israelite forces.

00:36:29.360 --> 00:36:39.735
One Israelite, he said, had chased a thousand, because God had fought for them. Then the speech became a warning. They must take care to love God.

00:36:39.915 --> 00:36:52.490
If they turned back, attached themselves to the remaining nations, and entered marriages and relationships that drew them into foreign worship, the nations would no longer be driven away.

00:36:52.670 --> 00:37:09.445
Instead they would become a trap and a snare, a scourge at their sides and thorns in their eyes, until Israel itself disappeared from the good land it had received. Joshua was close to death. He said he was going the way of all the earth.

00:37:09.625 --> 00:37:30.575
He asked the leaders to recognize that the good promises God had made had come true. But the fulfillment of those promises did not make the covenant unconditional. Just as the promised good had arrived, so could its threatened consequences if the people abandoned God and worshipped other deities. The land was a gift.

00:37:30.755 --> 00:37:47.530
Remaining in it was a responsibility. The warning echoed God's first command to Joshua decades earlier: be strong and courageous, and do not turn aside from the Torah. Now Joshua passed that command to the people who would outlive him.

00:37:48.230 --> 00:38:05.080
Joshua's final warning returns to the language of the book's opening: strength, courage, and fidelity to the Torah. The speech combines gratitude for fulfilled promises with an insistence that the people can still lose the land by violating the covenant.

00:38:05.260 --> 00:38:19.160
It also makes explicit that remaining Canaanite peoples have not all been expelled. The theological warning is specific to the narrative's covenantal framework, not a description of modern political relationships.

00:38:20.060 --> 00:38:22.760
Chapter 24. Choose.

00:38:23.860 --> 00:38:42.710
Joshua assembled Israel one last time at Shechem. He summoned the tribes, elders, heads, judges, and officers. They stood before God while the old leader recounted the history that had brought them to this land. The speech was not merely Joshua reminiscing.

00:38:42.890 --> 00:39:05.865
It was presented as a message from Israel's God, told in the first person. Their ancestors had once lived beyond the great river, the Euphrates. Terah, the father of Abraham and Nahor, had served other gods. God had taken Abraham from that world, led him through Canaan, multiplied his descendants, and given him Isaac.

00:39:06.045 --> 00:39:28.370
To Isaac had been born Jacob and Esau. Esau received the mountains of Seir; Jacob and his children went down to Egypt. The history moved forward. God sent Moses and Aaron. Egypt was struck. Israel was brought out of slavery, and when the Egyptian chariots pursued the refugees to the sea, God intervened.

00:39:28.550 --> 00:39:47.525
Darkness separated the two camps, the sea covered the pursuers, and Israel passed into the wilderness. They lived there for many days. Then God brought them into the territory of the Amorites east of the Jordan. Sihon and Og were defeated, and Israel took their land.

00:39:47.705 --> 00:40:09.105
Balak king of Moab tried to stop them through Balaam son of Beor, summoned to curse Israel. God did not permit the curse; Balaam blessed instead. Then came the Jordan. Jericho stood in their way, along with the peoples of Canaan: Amorites, Perizzites, Canaanites, Hittites, Girgashites, Hivites, and Jebusites.

00:40:09.285 --> 00:40:35.385
The victory, Joshua said in the divine voice, had not come from Israel's sword or bow alone. God said He had sent the hornet ahead of them to drive out enemies. Their victory was not something they could credit only to their weapons. He had given them a land they had not cleared, cities they had not built, and vineyards and olive groves they had not planted. The narrative had reached the present.

00:40:35.565 --> 00:40:54.440
Joshua stopped recounting and asked Israel to decide what kind of people it would be. They must fear God, serve Him sincerely, and put away the gods their ancestors had served beyond the Euphrates and in Egypt. Then Joshua posed the alternative without softening it.

00:40:54.620 --> 00:41:16.595
If serving Israel's God seemed wrong to them, they should choose that day whom they would serve: the gods their ancestors had worshipped, or the gods of the Amorites in whose land they now lived. Joshua's own choice was unambiguous. “As for me and my house, we will serve the Lord.” The people answered immediately.

00:41:16.775 --> 00:41:34.425
They would never abandon the God who had delivered them from Egypt, guarded them through the wilderness, and driven other peoples before them. Joshua did not simply congratulate them. “You cannot serve the Lord,” he warned, because God was holy and demanding.

00:41:34.605 --> 00:41:54.730
If they abandoned Him for other gods, the very God who had brought them good could bring judgment upon them. The people insisted again: they would serve the Lord. Joshua made them acknowledge the weight of their own words. They were witnesses against themselves, he said, that they had chosen whom to serve.

00:41:54.910 --> 00:42:13.460
“We are witnesses,” they answered. Then Joshua told them to remove the foreign gods still among them and turn their hearts toward the God of Israel. The people repeated their pledge: they would serve and obey Him. Joshua established a covenant with the people at Shechem.

00:42:13.640 --> 00:42:34.015
He set down a statute and ordinance and wrote the words in the book of God's law. Then he raised a large stone beneath an oak near the sanctuary. The stone, he told them, would stand as a witness. It had “heard” the words spoken there, and it would testify against them if they denied their God.

00:42:34.195 --> 00:42:51.720
It was the final public act of a leader who had spent his life carrying the nation from promise to inheritance. Joshua sent the people home, each to his own territory. After these events, Joshua son of Nun died at the age of 110.

00:42:51.900 --> 00:43:11.375
He was buried in his own inheritance at Timnath-serah, in the hill country of Ephraim, north of Mount Gaash. The book says that Israel remained faithful to God during Joshua's lifetime and through the years of the elders who outlived him and remembered the great events of the settlement.

00:43:11.555 --> 00:43:29.855
Two further burials bring the story to a close. The bones of Joseph, carried from Egypt by the Israelites, were buried at Shechem in the field Jacob had bought from the sons of Hamor for one hundred pieces of money. That land belonged to Joseph's descendants.

00:43:30.035 --> 00:43:49.035
Eleazar son of Aaron, the priest who had helped Joshua divide the land, also died. He was buried in the hill country of Ephraim, in the territory of his son Phinehas. Joshua was gone. Eleazar was gone. Joseph's remains had at last been laid to rest in the land.

00:43:49.215 --> 00:44:06.540
Three generations of Israel's story seemed to meet in those graves: the patriarchal promise, the Exodus, and the settlement of Canaan. But Joshua had left the people a question that no leader could answer for them permanently. Whom would they serve?

00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:27.465
Joshua's last speech retells Israel's history from Abraham through the conquest, including a striking acknowledgment that Abraham's ancestors worshipped other gods. Joshua then demands an explicit covenantal decision, repeatedly testing the people's declaration rather than accepting an easy promise.

00:44:27.645 --> 00:44:47.820
The final three burials matter: Joshua in his own allotment, Joseph's bones at Shechem, and Eleazar in Ephraim's hill country. The end of Joshua is therefore not merely the death of one hero. It closes the arc from Genesis and the Exodus while preparing for the unresolved future told in Judges.
